---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/guides/consent-compliance";
const title = "Shopify email consent: permission records and send decisions";
const desc = "Separate legal scope, capture evidence, channel eligibility and unsubscribe propagation in your Shopify email workflow.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does a purchase authorize every marketing channel?",
    "answer": "No operational rule should assume that. Determine eligibility by jurisdiction, sender, purpose and channel, with evidence for the applicable permission route."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is double opt-in a universal legal requirement?",
    "answer": "This guide makes no universal requirement claim. It can help verify address control, but the applicable rules and capture evidence still need review."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can an unsubscribe expire with a flow suppression?",
    "answer": "A temporary journey hold and a marketing withdrawal are different states. Do not reactivate marketing because a temporary hold expires."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/guides">Operator guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16 space-y-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Define the message before defining the audience</h2>
      <p>Create a register of message purpose, sender, channel, recipient type and applicable jurisdiction. A receipt, a promotional reorder reminder and an SMS campaign need separate decisions. Ask the responsible legal reviewer to document the applicable rule and any exception, including its limits. A tool badge or checked box does not certify the entire program.</p>
      <p>This guide gives an operating process and scoped US and UK references, not a complete international legal assessment. For EU member states, Canada, Australia or US state-specific and SMS requirements, obtain an applicable review before enabling that branch. Do not use shipping country alone as conclusive evidence of every law that applies.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>US commercial email: keep CAN-SPAM distinct</h2>
      <p>The FTC describes CAN-SPAM requirements for US commercial email, including accurate headers and subjects, advertising identification, a valid postal address and an accessible opt-out. It states opt-outs must be honored within ten business days and the mechanism remain available for at least thirty days after sending. These are legal limits, not a reason to delay suppression.</p>
      <p>The FTC also distinguishes transactional or relationship messages by primary purpose; an existing customer relationship does not automatically make a message transactional. Review mixed promotional and service content. This summary concerns commercial email under CAN-SPAM, not permission to send marketing texts or a conclusion about other applicable laws. See the linked FTC guide for its full scope.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>UK individual recipients: document the specific route</h2>
      <p>The ICO describes specific consent for electronic mail marketing to individuals, with a limited existing-customer exception for the sender’s own similar products or services when opt-out opportunities were provided at collection and in each message. Sole traders and some partnerships are treated as individuals. A corporate recipient needs a separate assessment, including personal-data use.</p>
      <p>A general data-protection lawful basis does not by itself settle electronic-marketing permission. Record the conditions relied on rather than importing all purchasers as eligible. The ICO marks this PECR guidance as under review following the Data (Use and Access) Act; check the current guidance when approving a campaign. These UK conditions should not be generalized to every European country.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Make capture evidence survive a redesign</h2>
      <p>Inventory checkout, newsletter footer, popups, landing pages, contests, partner referrals and imports. For each, save the exact disclosure version, channel choice, sender identity, purpose, source and timestamp. Record the person’s affirmative action where consent is the chosen route. Keep incentive fulfilment separate from an assumption that entry grants marketing permission.</p>
      <p>When a form changes, issue a new version instead of overwriting what earlier subscribers saw. Check mobile layouts and translated disclosures. A theme deployment or app replacement should trigger a capture-to-profile rehearsal. Double opt-in can help verify address control and evidence, but do not label it universally required or sufficient for every jurisdiction.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Use explicit states rather than a single audience tag</h2>
      <p>Maintain channel-specific states such as unknown, pending confirmation, eligible, withdrawn and operationally held. Preserve the supporting evidence and the rule applied. A customer record or order is not an eligibility state. Do not convert a blank imported field into consent, or use an email subscription as SMS authorization.</p>
      <p>An eligibility decision should consider purpose and sender as well as channel. Store the event time and origin so a stale import cannot overwrite a newer withdrawal. Where records conflict, quarantine the affected marketing branch for investigation. Temporary collision holds may expire; a withdrawal must not expire with them.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Propagate withdrawal to every sending path</h2>
      <p>Identify the system receiving the withdrawal, the durable suppression record and all consumers: campaigns, queued flows, capture tools, service tools with promotional features and any native sender still enabled. Apply the stop promptly at the sender rather than waiting for a nightly sync. Recheck eligibility at dispatch so messages already queued do not escape.</p>
      <p>Handle partial preferences without hiding a way to stop all marketing. Record acknowledgements and failed propagation, with an owner who can pause an affected sender. Test a withdrawal arriving during import and a later purchase that must not silently resubscribe the person. Re-entry requires a valid new permission event under the applicable rules, not a tag reset.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Keep SMS and service communications separately owned</h2>
      <p>Collect and evaluate SMS permission through its own reviewed flow; phone numbers gathered for delivery are not automatically a marketing audience. Have the reviewer cover the applicable texting rules, revocation routes and sending restrictions for the market and technology in use. Do not copy quiet-hour defaults from another country or label a platform TCPA-compliant as a blanket conclusion.</p>
      <p>Service communications need their own purpose review and suppression behavior. A marketing opt-out should not be treated as permission to add offers to a shipping notice. Give support a route for mistaken identity and unwanted messages, and have legal or privacy owners resolve cases involving deletion, retention and required service notices.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Migrate the stop records before the eligible audience</h2>
      <p>Export permission evidence and withdrawal history along with active profiles. Map each source state explicitly, preserve event times and reconcile exceptions by identifier. Start with non-sending imports. Compare actual eligible and suppressed populations, not only total profile counts; equal totals can conceal reversed states.</p>
      <p>Propose staff scenarios for email-only permission, pending confirmation, SMS-only permission, an unsubscribed purchaser and withdrawal during cutover. Inspect each destination state and queue before assigning sending ownership. These are exercises for the merchant to perform, not results observed here. Never run duplicate live campaigns as a migration validation method.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Resolve missing evidence without a cold re-permission blast</h2>
      <p>If an old list has no usable permission evidence, isolate it from marketing and investigate its source and applicable rules. Being a previous opener is not a substitute for permission. A request to renew permission can itself be marketing, so do not send it merely because the record is old or the person has not explicitly unsubscribed.</p>
      <p>Keep operational suppression distinct from deletion. Ask the privacy owner to define what evidence and minimal stop records to retain, for what purpose and duration, and how requests are handled across processors. Retaining everything indefinitely and deleting every stop record both create operational problems; the policy needs a documented decision.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Make the handoff reviewable</h2>
      <p>For each active branch, give the operator a purpose statement, rule reference, approved capture version, eligibility query, withdrawal route, propagation owner and pause control. Require candidates, including Sequenzy, to demonstrate these records and export paths in the proposed configuration. No supplier ranking substitutes for that inspection.</p>
      <p>Track missing-evidence cases, propagation failures and unwanted-message reports by source. Review after form, integration or market changes and when an exception appears. Do not promise improved inbox placement from a permission fix: consent operations and deliverability monitoring inform each other but answer different questions.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl"><h2>Primary references and scope</h2><p>Reviewed 25 November 2026: <a class="link" href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/can-spam-act-compliance-guide-business">FTC commercial email guide</a> and <a class="link" href="https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/direct-marketing-and-privacy-and-electronic-communications/guide-to-pecr/electronic-and-telephone-marketing/electronic-mail-marketing/">ICO electronic mail marketing guidance</a>. Recheck the applicable authority when rules, markets or message purposes change.</p></div>
  </section>
  <nav class="container pb-12 flex flex-wrap gap-4" aria-label="Related guidance">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">App evaluations</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/consent-compliance">Consent</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/deliverability">Deliverability</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/discount-discipline">Discount discipline</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/migration">Migration</a>
  </nav>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
